      "id": "finding-1-core-conclusion",
      "text": "Core Conclusion/核心结论: The immediate failure is that scripts/check_eol.py exits non-zero because proxy_gateway/app.py contains at least one CRLF line ending, while the repository policy requires LF-only for tracked text files. The complete fix plan is: (1) change proxy_gateway/app.py to LF-only line endings, (2) update or run the repository's normalization tooling (e.g., .gitattributes with text eol=lf, git add --renormalize) so future checkouts do not re-introduce CRLF, (3) migration impact is limited to the single file; no runtime logic changes, but the file's byte content changes, so any open uncommitted patches or diffs against that file may need rebasing, (4) rollback path is to restore the previous CRLF version via git revert or git checkout <old-commit> -- proxy_gateway/app.py, then re-run the EOL check to confirm the old failure state returns (or a backup copy is restored), and (5) one falsifiable acceptance check is: after applying the fix, run `scripts/check_eol.py` and assert it exits with status 0 and prints no 'mixed line endings' or 'CRLF' diagnostic for proxy_gateway/app.py; conversely, the check must fail (non-zero exit) if a CRLF is artificially re-introduced into that file, proving the check is sensitive and not a no-op.",
      "layer": "L3",
      "evidence": [
        "User states that scripts/check_eol.py fails with message 'proxy_gateway/app.py: mixed line endings (CRLF)'",
        "User states repo policy is LF-only for tracked text"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "finding-2-epistemic-breakdown",
      "text": "Epistemic Breakdown/认识论分解: The statement 'proxy_gateway/app.py has mixed line endings CRLF' is directly observed evidence from the failing tool output; that is the weakest honest layer L2/L3 (tool observation, not yet independently verified by the agent, but accepted as given). From that, the causal model is: the repository's enforcement script parses tracked text files and detects any CRLF bytes; therefore line-ending normalization is required. This is a valid inference but depends on the script's exact detection logic, which the agent has not read in full; so the fix plan is honest at L3 (domain-established practice) rather than L4 (verified implementation). The claim that 'only one file is affected' is NOT established; the failed run may stop at the first file or list only one file in the user's message; therefore migration impact is scoped only to the reported file plus a re-normalization pass that could touch other files if the script reports more after the first fix. A specific applicable scope and counterexample: the LF-only policy applies to tracked text files; a counterexample is generated files (e.g., compiled artifacts) or binary files that may legitimately contain CRLF bytes; the fix must exclude those, e.g., via .gitattributes marking them as binary. Another counterexample: files added after the fix with CRLF from a Windows check-out must be re-normalized; so the acceptance test must additionally create a temporary CRLF file and verify the script catches it, otherwise the check may be passing due to a stale cache or skipped path.",
      "layer": "L3",
      "evidence": [
        "User-provided error message",
        "Common repository practice for LF-only checks (gitattributes, eol directives)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "finding-3-evidence-gaps",
      "text": "Evidence Gaps/证据缺口: (a) The exact content of scripts/check_eol.py is not inspected; it may check only staged files, only the working tree, or only certain extensions; thus the acceptance test must run against both the working tree and a staged version (`git add` then run) to cover the enforcement scope. (b) Whether other files also contain CRLF is unknown; the plan should include running the full `scripts/check_eol.py` after the first fix and, if more files are reported, repeat normalization across all of them under the same policy. (c) The repository's .gitattributes absence/presence is unknown; if absent, the plan must create one with `* text=auto` and `*.py text eol=lf`; if present, the plan must verify the Python pattern. (d) The CI integration point (pre-commit hook or CI job) is unknown; the plan should include ensuring the same check runs in CI, otherwise the fix is not durable. (e) The exact command that produced the failure (e.g., plain `python scripts/check_eol.py` vs `git diff --check`) is not confirmed; but the user explicitly named scripts/check_eol.py so the plan targets that script.",
      "layer": "L3",
      "evidence": [
        "No direct access to repo contents",
        "User provided only the failing script name and policy"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "finding-4-complete-fix-plan",
      "text": "Complete fix plan (applies to the named file; generalize if more files appear):\n1. Change point(s):\n   - Convert proxy_gateway/app.py to LF-only: run `python -c \"from pathlib import Path; p=Path('proxy_gateway/app.py'); data=p.read_bytes(); data=data.replace(b'\\r\\n', b'\\n').replace(b'\\r', b'\\n'); p.write_bytes(data)\"` (this also normalizes any lone CR to LF). If the file contains intent to preserve CR bytes in string literals, adjust accordingly; but for a Python source file, CR-only inside string literals is pathological and should be removed under the policy.\n   - Ensure git is told the file is text and LF: create or edit `.gitattributes` to add: `proxy_gateway/app.py text eol=lf` (or broader: `*.py text eol=lf` if policy is all Python files). Then run `git add --renormalize .` so the index stores LF canonical form.\n   - Optionally fix the script's detection logic if it currently prints 'mixed line endings' but does not identify which bytes; but the user asked for a fix to the failing file, not to modify the checker. If the checker has a bug (e.g., flags a file that is all-LF due to a Unicode BOM containing CR? unlikely), then after normalizing you may need to harden the script; but do not change the checker until the acceptance test proves the checker is correct.\n   - Add a pre-commit hook or CI step to run `scripts/check_eol.py` so future CRLF introductions fail immediately.\n2. Migration impact:\n   - Only byte-level changes to proxy_gateway/app.py (and possibly other files if `.gitattributes` renormalization touches them). No change to runtime behavior because Python source semantics for line endings are identical after normalization; however, if there are string literals that contain `\\r\\n` escape sequences, those are ASCII backslash sequences and are unaffected by line-ending normalization. If there are raw multi-line string literals, content inside them may change if they were literally written with CRLF bytes inside the triple-quoted block; that could alter string values at runtime. Audit such multi-line string literals in proxy_gateway/app.py before finalizing; if any exist, adjust their content deliberately.\n   - Developers on Windows who open the file in editors with CRLF auto-conversion may see diff noise unless .gitattributes is committed before/with the file rewrite. Mitigation: commit .gitattributes first, then `git add --renormalize`, then the working tree on Windows will check out LF (Git handles it).\n   - Git history comparisons: the blob for proxy_gateway/app.py will change; any open branch with edits to that file will have a merge conflict or require `git rebase`; that is acceptable but should be communicated.\n3. Rollback path:\n   - Before changing, record the current blob SHA: `git rev-parse HEAD:proxy_gateway/app.py`. Then to rollback: `git checkout <previous-commit> -- proxy_gateway/app.py`; or if you want to restore exact CRLF content, keep a backup copy (e.g., `cp proxy_gateway/app.py /tmp/app.py.crlf.bak`) before normalization. After rollback, run `scripts/check_eol.py` again; it should fail again, confirming the rollback restored the pre-fix state. If you also added .gitattributes, rollback by `git revert <commit-of-gitattributes>` and then `git add --renormalize` to undo index normalization.\n4. Falsifiable acceptance check:\n   - Let S be the script `scripts/check_eol.py`. Define acceptance predicate A: after the fix, `S` exits 0 and `S` prints nothing about proxy_gateway/app.py. To prove the check is not vacuous, execute a controlled test: (i) create a temp copy of the fixed file with a CRLF introduced: `python -c \"data=open('proxy_gateway/app.py','rb').read().replace(b'\\n', b'\\r\\n'); open('/tmp/app_crlf.py','wb').write(data)\"`; (ii) run `S` against that temp copy (if the script accepts a path argument) or temporarily overwrite the file, run S, and then restore the LF file; assert S exits non-zero. If the script has no path argument, the test overwrites the real file, so do it in a separate test worktree (e.g., `git stash` or `git worktree add`) to avoid corrupting the fix. The acceptance check is falsifiable: if S exits 0 even with a CRLF present, the fix is incomplete or the checker is ineffective; then revise the plan. Also include a durability check: re-run `git diff --check` and verify no trailing whitespace/line-ending noise; and re-run `scripts/check_eol.py` from a fresh clone (CI) to prove the repo state is clean independent of local conversion settings.",
      "layer": "L3",
      "evidence": [
        "Standard git line-ending normalization procedures",
        "User policy statement"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    { "id": "user-provided-error", "kind": "direct-user-statement" },
    { "id": "repo-policy-lf-only", "kind": "direct-user-statement" },
    { "id": "gitattributes-common-practice", "kind": "external-common-knowledge" }
  ],
  "confidence_cap": "L3",
  "gaps": [
    "Exact source of scripts/check_eol.py not read; detection scope (working tree vs index vs full repo) unverified; acceptance test must be adapted to the script's actual CLI (path argument or none).",
    "Unknown whether other tracked files also contain CRLF; plan's initial change point is only the reported file; full renormalization of the repo may be needed. The plan includes a follow-up run to discover that.",
    "Unknown .gitattributes current state; if missing, creating `*.py text eol=lf` may affect more files than expected; use `git status --short` after renormalize to audit the diff.",
    "Unknown if the file has raw multi-line string literals containing literal CRLF bytes; those would change runtime string values after normalization; audit required before committing.",
    "Unknown CI or pre-commit integration; the acceptance test alone will not enforce future compliance unless the check is wired into CI."
  ],
  "recommendation": "Proceed with the fix but in this exact order: (1) backup the file and record the current blob SHA; (2) create/update .gitattributes and commit it separately; (3) normalize proxy_gateway/app.py to LF; (4) run `git add --renormalize` and inspect the diff; (5) run `scripts/check_eol.py` and verify exit 0; (6) run the anti-test with a deliberately CRLF-corrupted file to prove the checker triggers non-zero; (7) if more files are reported, repeat normalization; (8) wire `scripts/check_eol.py` into CI/pre-commit if not already present; (9) document the rollback command in the MR description and keep the backup until CI passes on a fresh clone."
}

