    "Git config values: core.autocrlf, core.eol",
    "Whether the CRLF is in the index already, or only in the working tree"
  ],
  "recommendation": "Fix plan:\n\n1. Change points:\n   - Normalize proxy_gateway/app.py to LF only:\n     `sed -i 's/\\r$//' proxy_gateway/app.py`\n     or `dos2unix proxy_gateway/app.py`.\n   - If the repo uses a .gitattributes with `* text=auto`, alternatively run:\n     `git add --renormalize proxy_gateway/app.py`\n     (this normalizes only this file, avoiding accidental changes to other files).\n   - Stage the change: `git add proxy_gateway/app.py`.\n   - Optionally add/update .gitattributes to enforce LF for tracked text, e.g. `* text=auto eol=lf` (but only if repo policy explicitly wants a global guard).\n\n2. Migration impact:\n   - The file bytes change from CRLF to LF on disk; commit hash of the file changes; any diff against the previous commit will show the whole line endings changed if the prior commit stored CRLF.\n   - If the current index already had CRLF, the staged version will be LF after `git add`.\n   - No runtime behavior change for Python code, because LF vs CRLF does not affect Python semantics (except inside string literals with embedded `\\r\\n`, which are not expected here).\n   - Other files are untouched, so no impact on them.\n\n3. Rollback path:\n   - Before committing: `git checkout -- proxy_gateway/app.py` restores the CRLF version from HEAD.\n   - After committing: `git revert <commit>` or `git reset --hard HEAD~1` (if not pushed), then re-apply any real code changes if mixed in the same commit.\n   - To clean the index only: `git reset HEAD proxy_gateway/app.py`.\n\n4. Falsifiable acceptance check:\n   - Run `python scripts/check_eol.py`; it must exit with code 0 and produce no output mentioning CRLF.\n   - Run `git ls-files --eol proxy_gateway/app.py`; the output must contain `i/lf` and `w/lf`.\n   - Run `grep -P '\\r' proxy_gateway/app.py`; it must find no matches (exit code 1, i.e. no CR bytes).\n   - All three must pass; if any one fails, the fix is not complete."
